    "media:imagetext": "12. 4 64\nBEiRUT\nDear F\nand F.,\nFriday Dec. 4th, 1964.\nWe were invited to a very fine dinner last\nnight by a V-P of the Continental Oil Co. It was good to meet a\nsizeable group of the American community. This morning Debby went\nto the \"SUQ\" with some ladies she met at dinner.\n\"SUQ\" is the\nArabic word for bazaar. She bought a bracelet and the next day,\nSat. Dec. 5th, she talked me into going around. You'd know that\nyou were not in Tiffany's. that evening we had dinner with the\nBoyntons and the Bojonells, he is PAA #1 in Athens and was over here\non a brief business\ntrip.\nWe were glad to see them again. Boynton\nhad just come bask from Cyprus.\nHe said externally at least, Cyprus\nnever had it so good. Every hotel and cafe full every night with\nfree-spending United Nations people, Army officers and newspaper\nreporters from all over the world.\nSun. Dec. 6th.\nI have stayed in bed all day trying unsuccessfully\nto shake a cold. Tomorrow we leave for Hong Kong. I feel a little\nsad at not going to 8,000 foot level Teheran and some what lower\nbut colder Afghanastan as we had more or less planned. Guess I am\nnot tough like my old friend Phil Ball. No doubt most of you have\nheard this story but a good story is always worth hepeating ( if you\ndon't do it too often).\nBack in 1927 Mr. Ball bought a Ryan airplane\nHe asked me to recommend a pilot and I did. This particular man,\nFrank Ryan Dunn, was about as homely and as tough as they come but\na good safe pilot.\nWhen I sent Frank up to Mr. Ball's office for an\ninterview I said \"Frank, make it perfectly clear right from the\nstart that Mr. Ball is boss on the ground bøt that you are boss in the\nsky as well as deciding whether or not you should venture therein\".\nFrank went on upstairs and about 20 minutes later came back by my\ndesk in the old State National Bank. He said \" Gee, I've met some\ntough guys but we made a deal, we\"ll get along all right\". Another\nfive minutes and Mr. Ball came down \"Say Bix, where in h---_ did\nyou find that porch climber?\" They were together until Mr. Ball's\ndeath (in bed) some 15 years later.\nWhen the Ryan was ready for\ndelivery they went out to San Diego together.. Frank was checked\nout on the plane and they took off for El Paso. Mr. Ball said\n\"Frank, climb up to 12,000 feet\".\nFrank demurred because it was a\nslow climb with a full load but he went on up to 12,000 feet and\nran \"down hill\" into El Paso. As they were having dinner together\nthat night Frank said \"Mr. Ball why did you want me to go to 12,000\nfeet?\"\nMr. Ball said \"Well Frank, I didn't want to worrys you\nbut\nI don't mind telling you now. My doctor told me h*sth that if I\nwent up to 10,000 feet I'd drop dead. I knew that he didn't know\nwhat he was talking about so I spotted him 2000 feet and as yau\nknow I'm still around\".\nWell I just do not have as much nerve as\nold man Ball. When perhaps you can't get enough air to keep the\nold pump going its better to play safe. I had another reason for\ngoing to Teheran.\nYears ago in New York, I got a call from a\nWhite House contact asking me to meet a Mr. Pahlevi at theairport,\nshow him around, give him a good time, \"quite important\" Washington\nsaid.\nSo I met Mr. Pahdevi who was a most friendly youngen,\nWe\nwent through the hangars, he tried the link trainer, and did quite\nwell, we had the usual buffet lunch where what you don't spill is\nyour own with a group of executives and piots and the young man\nwhose Oxford English was better tire",
